    </li>
    <li>
        This new covenant was instituted by Jesus, who fulfilled the prophecy through His atoning death, 
        burial, and resurrection. The new covenant was ratified at the last supper when Christ said,
        
        <i>"This is my body, which is given for you. Do this in remembrance of me. This cup that is poured out for 
        you is the new covenant in my blood. Do this, as often as you drink it, in remembrance of me."</i>
    </li>
    <li>
        Christ became the great high priest of the new covenant, fulfilling the Levitical
        priesthood; he also became the perfect, once-for-all sacrifice, fulfilling the temple sacrifices.
    </li>
    <li>
        Because the promises of the new covenant are spiritual, faith is required to receive them. By faith the
        people of God are forgiven of their sins, united to Christ, and raised to new life; likewise, unbelief 
        cuts one off from the new covenant and from Christ.
    </li>
    <li>
        These new promises also require new and better sacraments: circumcision was
        succeeded by baptism, and the passover was
        succeeded by the Lord's Supper.
    </li>
    <li>
        Because the new covenant is better than the old, the new sacraments are also more powerful than the old.
        Where circumcision of the flesh was an outward sign, circumcision of the heart in baptism bestows the 
        forgiveness of sins and indwelling of Holy Spirit. Where the passover was a remembrance, the Lord's
        supper brings sanctification and communion with Christ.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />